 3. 🐊 The Florida Man primary 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios' Marc Caputo, a Miami man and Miami Herald alumnus, writes from Tallahassee, Fla.: 

 Today is the Florida Man primary. "It's really weird," one political pro said.

 Why it matters: Florida, MAGA's beating heart and President Trump's home, was a key prize in his redistricting push to add more GOP-friendly congressional seats. 🖼️ The big picture: What makes some of the contests stand out isn't their competitiveness — it's how they evoke the wacky Florida Man internet meme.

 These campaigns featured carpetbaggers, racial conflict, homophobia, struggling socialists, antisemitism, and even a scandal-plagued member of Congress slapped with a restraining order. "It's like Florida Man is having his own election right now," said a Republican political consultant financing one of the state's marquee races. The Sunshine State's key showdowns: 

 The governor's race: Rep. Byron Donalds is expected to cruise to a win in the GOP primary. One opponent — James Fishback — has generated social media buzz and mainstream media coverage, partly with racist dog whistles . Donalds is on the cusp of becoming the GOP's first Black nominee for Florida governor and would be the favorite to become the state's first Black governor . 2. The Senate race: Democrats' consensus view is that likely nominee Alex Vindman has run such a lackluster campaign that he may come uncomfortably close to his underfunded left-wing opponent, state Rep. Angie Nixon.

 3. The House races offer some of Florida's strangest drama:

 In the 6th District, Rep. Randy Fine faces a former "manosphere" influencer who has drawn bipartisan condemnation for antisemitic attacks. Scandal-plagued Rep. Cory Mills is fighting for his political survival in the 7th District after being snubbed by Trump. Gov. Ron DeSantis (R) also said he doesn't support Mills. In the 20th District, former Rep. Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick is seeking her old seat after resigning under federal indictment. Rep. Debbie Wasserman Schultz could benefit from a split among four Black candidates in the historically Black-held district. In the heavily Jewish 25th District, Rep. Jared Moskowitz faces an anti-Zionist democratic socialist in a proxy fight between the pro-Israel establishment and the progressive left. Keep reading . 

 
 
