 4. 📉 Biggest violent-crime drop in 90 years 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data : FBI. (2021 data was estimated due to a low number of agencies reporting.) Chart: Noah Bressner/Axios Axios' Russell Contreras, who closely tracks criminal justice data, writes: 

 The U.S. violent crime rate plunged 9.7% last year — the largest annual drop since the FBI's national estimates began in 1936 — according to the bureau's data released last week.

 Why it matters: The data underscore the historic scale of America's crime decline — a trend that appears to be continuing in the first half of this year. 💡 Between the lines: Experts note that the nation's violent crime rate (now the lowest since 1956 ) has been dropping since 2022 . The reasons for the decrease, which is widespread geographically , are unclear: Rates are falling in cities with different public safety strategies, economic conditions and political leadership.

 A leading theory: U.S. drug overdoses , alcohol consumption and firearms purchases — which helped spike the homicide rate when they were going up — are all down. And post-COVID , cities returned to more stable routines and employment. President Trump takes credit for the decline, pointing to his National Guard deployments in D.C. , Memphis and other cities. 🧮 By the numbers: Murder and nonnegligent manslaughter dropped 18.1%. 

 Robbery fell 18.5%, rape 7.6% and aggravated assault 7.2%. The U.S. murder rate decreased 18.5% to 4.1 per 100,000 residents, tying 1955 and 1956 for the lowest rate since FBI national estimates began. Property crime declined 12.4%, led by drops in motor vehicle theft (22.7%), burglary (15.8%) and larceny-theft (9.8%). Share this story . 

 
 
